+/// Farhelm patch: the most OSC payload bytes the `std` parser buffers for one
+/// sequence, counting every byte it buffers: those between `ESC ]` and the
+/// terminator, except the `;` separators and the C0 controls it ignores.
+///
+/// Upstream buffers an OSC string in a `Vec` with no limit, so one huge or
+/// unterminated sequence grows the embedder's memory by whatever the program
+/// sends. Once a sequence reaches this many bytes the parser stops buffering
+/// it, and when it ends the whole sequence is dropped rather than dispatched
+/// truncated (a truncated OSC 52 would put corrupt data on the clipboard). The
+/// performer hears of the drop through [`Perform::osc_overflow`] instead.
+///
+/// The value sits just above xterm.js's limit of 10,000,000 payload characters,
+/// so every OSC 52 clipboard write that xterm.js accepted still works. The
+/// `no_std` parser keeps its own fixed-size buffer and is not affected.
+#[cfg(feature = "std")]
+pub const OSC_PAYLOAD_LIMIT: usize = 10 * 1024 * 1024;
