+    /// Spec: `osc_overflow` carries exactly the parameters that a `;` ended
+    /// before the payload reached `OSC_PAYLOAD_LIMIT`: none when the first
+    /// parameter itself is too long, and never the parameter that was being
+    /// received at the limit or any after it, even ones that end later.
+    ///
+    /// Why: the terminal ends an active hyperlink when it hears that an OSC 8
+    /// was dropped, so the command number has to arrive whenever it was
+    /// complete; and any parameter cut off at the limit is truncated data,
+    /// which must never reach the performer.
